    "title": "Sneap + Allom: Modern Clarity Meets Classic Priest",
    "content": "*Firepower*'s production was the headline gear story of the album. Andy Sneap, who had become one of metal's most in-demand modern producers through his work with Accept, Exodus, Testament, and Megadeth, handled the bulk of the engineering and contemporary production duties at his own Old Mill Recording Studios. Alongside him was Tom Allom — the producer behind *British Steel* (1980), *Point of Entry* (1981), *Screaming for Vengeance* (1982), *Defenders of the Faith* (1984), *Turbo* (1986), *Priest...Live!* (1987), and *Ram It Down* (1988). Allom had not produced a Priest studio album in thirty years.\n\n**Recording Environment:**\nThe drums were tracked primarily at Old Mill Recording Studios — Sneap's facility — with additional sessions at Witchroom Sound. Old Mill is built around a controlled live room with adjustable acoustic treatment, allowing Sneap to dial in either tight, dead-room drum sounds or open, ambient ones depending on the song. For *Firepower*, the room was tuned tight — the album's drum sound is dry and present, with minimal room wash, putting Travis's kit directly in the listener's face.\n\n**Microphone Setup:**\n- Kick drums: Shure Beta 91A inside, Audix D6 outside, Yamaha Subkick for low-end weight\n- Snare: Shure SM57 top, Sennheiser e904 bottom\n- Toms: Sennheiser e904 on each\n- Hi-hat: AKG C451B\n- Overheads: Neumann KM184s in spaced pair\n- Room mics: Neumann U87s, used minimally\n\n**The Sneap-Allom Production Dynamic:**\nSneap handled the engineering, mixing, and modern production techniques — sample blending on kick drums for consistency, careful EQ to slot the drums alongside the dual-guitar wall, and precise transient shaping on the snare. Allom contributed arrangement perspective and the classic Priest sensibility for song dynamics — when to pull back the drums, when to let them dominate, when the snare should sit forward versus blend into the wall. The result is a drum sound that is unmistakably modern in clarity but unmistakably Priest in feel.\n\n**Travis's Performance Approach:**\nBy 2018 Travis was thirty years into his Priest tenure, and his studio approach reflects that maturity. The performances on *Firepower* are precise without being mechanical, powerful without being overplayed. Sneap captured the takes with minimal editing — Travis's consistency at this stage of his career makes heavy editing unnecessary.\n\n**Mix Philosophy:**\nThe drums sit prominently in the *Firepower* mix, but with a different character than *Painkiller*'s aggressive dryness. Where *Painkiller* placed the drums right against the listener with razor-sharp attack, *Firepower* gives them more weight and harmonic depth while preserving the upfront placement. It is the *Painkiller* drum philosophy refined for modern listening environments.",
