  "conclusion": {
    "title": "The Engine Room at Thirty Years In",
    "content": "*Firepower* arrived in March 2018 as a celebration of Judas Priest's longevity and a vindication of the band's modern-era lineup. The Grammy nomination, the UK Gold certification, the #5 chart positions on both sides of the Atlantic — these were not nostalgia metrics. They were earned by the album's actual quality, and a meaningful share of that quality was Scott Travis's drumming.\n\nFor Travis, *Firepower* marked his thirty-year tenure milestone. The kid from Norfolk, Virginia who had cut his teeth with Racer X and replaced Dave Holland in 1989 was now the longest-serving non-founding member of one of metal's most important bands. Eighteen studio Priest albums had passed since *Painkiller* — and yet Travis was still bringing the same combination of speed, power, precision, and musicality that earned him the job in the first place.\n\nThe rig from *Painkiller* to *Firepower* tells a story of consistency, not evolution: the same ddrum Dominion Series shell pack, the same ddrum Scott Travis Signature snare, the same Paiste RUDE/2002 cymbal voice, the same DW 9000 Series double pedal. And the philosophy underneath has not changed either. Tight bass-drum tuning for articulation. A signature snare built for cut. Two independent bass drums. Cymbals that project and define. Heel-up technique with natural rebound. Speed built on relaxation, power built on full technique.\n\nFor drummers studying *Firepower*, the lessons extend beyond gear. The album shows what a metal drummer's career can look like at thirty years in — not a fading shadow of past glory, but a fully evolved version of the same playing identity. The speed is still there. The precision is still there. The musicality is still there. What is added is the maturity to deploy each of those qualities exactly when the song requires it.\n\nAndy Sneap's modern production gave Travis's playing the contemporary clarity it deserved. Tom Allom's classic-Priest perspective ensured the arrangement choices honored the band's history. Together they delivered an album that was both a present-tense statement and a long-arc payoff for the listener who had followed Travis from *Painkiller* through *Nostradamus* to here.\n\n*Firepower* did not just match *Painkiller*'s chart performance. It demonstrated that the philosophy behind Travis's drumming — and the philosophy behind Judas Priest itself — could still produce essential heavy metal twenty-eight years later. The engine room was still running."
  },
